Since you're on .NET 10, you don't need a custom wrapper — use the **built-in `TimeProvider`** abstraction.

### 1. Register in DI (`Program.cs` / `Startup.cs`)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

### 2. Inject into your class

```csharp
public class ReportGenerator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public Report Generate()
    {
        var now = timeProvider.GetUtcNow(); // replaces DateTime.UtcNow
        // ...
    }
}
```

### 3. Test with `FakeTimeProvider`

Install the testing helper:

```
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

Then in your test:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing;

var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 3, 12, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var generator = new ReportGenerator(fakeTime);
var report = generator.Generate();

Assert.Equal(new DateTime(2026, 7, 3, 12, 0, 0), report.GeneratedAt);

// You can also advance time:
fakeTime.Advance(TimeSpan.FromHours(1));
```

### Key points

- **`TimeProvider`** is built into .NET 8+ — no extra NuGet for production code.
- **`FakeTimeProvider`** (from `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`) gives you full control: set a fixed time, advance it, etc.
- Replace every `DateTime.UtcNow` → `timeProvider.GetUtcNow()` and every `DateTime.Now` → `timeProvider.GetLocalNow()`.

